rtance\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"number\",\n        \"std\": 0.2106873338543795,\n        \"min\": 0.005489771146312314,\n        \"max\": 0.6891159400358505,\n        \"num_unique_values\": 10,\n        \"samples\": [\n          0.00777910465613579,\n          0.0964775539889791,\n          0.0140274589574313\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}"}},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"55aeeb37"},"source":["### **PHASE 9: Model Serialization and Deployment API**\n","\n","In this section, we save the trained pipeline, design a robust Flask REST API that validates incoming data, run it locally within our Colab environment, and send a sample payload to demonstrate its capabilities."]},{"cell_type":"code","metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"ab8d734b","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1794479038559,"user_tz":-60,"elapsed":50,"user":{"displayName":"THERESA TERFA","userId":"01331411577232298609"}},"outputId":"2d9d48e0-032e-4289-b2ea-c67c90cd71f7"},"source":["import joblib\n","\n","# 1. Save the best-performing model pipeline (the Tuned Gradient Boosting model)\n","model_filename = 'churn_prediction_pipeline.joblib'\n","joblib.dump(best_gb_model, model_filename)\n","print(f\"Model pipeline successfully saved to: {model_filename}\")"],"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Model pipeline successfully saved to: churn_prediction_pipeline.joblib\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"13c0ed43","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1794479044315,"user_tz":-60,"elapsed":24,"user":{"displayName":"THERESA TERFA","userId":"01331411577232298609"}},"outputId":"1ecf2fb4-40de-4096-b656-408c212ef075"},"source":["%%writefile app.py\n","from flask import Flask, request, jsonify\n","import joblib\n","import pandas as pd\n","\n","app = Flask(__name__)\n","\n","# Load the pre-trained pipeline securely\n","try:\n","    pipeline = joblib.load('churn_prediction_pipeline.joblib')\n","    print(\"Model loaded successfully!\")\n","except Exception as e:\n","    print(f\"Error loading model: {e}\")\n","    pipeline = None\n","\n","@app.route('/predict', methods=['POST'])\n","def predict():\n","    if pipeline is None:\n","        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Model is not initialized or loaded.\"}), 500\n","\n","    data = request.get_json(silent=True)\n","    if not data:\n","        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Invalid input: No JSON payload provided.\"}), 400\n","\n","    # Ensure data is formatted as a list of records or single record\n","    if isinstance(data, dict):\n","        records = [data]\n","    elif isinstance(data, list):\n","        records = data\n","    else:\n","        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Invalid input format. Must be a JSON object or array.\"}), 400\n","\n","    # Required feature fields\n","    required_fields = [\n","        'gender', 'state', 'membership_tier', 'preferred_channel', 'referral_source',\n","        'frequency', 'total_spend', 'avg_spend_per_order',\n","        'ticket_count', 'avg_satisfaction', 'avg_resolution_time'\n","    ]\n","\n","    # Validate and build DataFrame\n","    cleaned_records = []\n","    for i, r in enumerate(records):\n","        cleaned_record = {}\n","        for field in required_fields:\n","            if field not in r:\n","                return jsonify({\"error\": f\"Missing required field '{field}' in record {i}.\"}), 400\n","            cleaned_record[field] = r[field]\n","        cleaned_records.append(cleaned_record)\n","\n","    try:\n","        # Convert to Pandas DataFrame matching model input expectations\n","        df_input = pd.DataFrame(cleaned_records)\n","\n","        # Generate predictions and probabilities\n","        predictions = pipeline.predict(df_input).tolist()\n","        probabilities = pipeline.predict_proba(df_input)[:, 1].tolist()\n","\n","        response = []\n","        for pred, prob in zip(predictions, probabilities):\n","            response.append({\n","                \"is_churned_prediction\": int(pred),\n","                \"churn_probability\": round(float(prob), 4),\n","                \"status\": \"Churn\" if pred == 1 else \"Retained\"\n","            })\n","\n","        return jsonify({\"results\": response})\n","\n","    except Exception as e:\n","        return jsonify({\"error\": f\"An error occurred during inference: {str(e)}\"}), 500\n","\n","if __name__ == '__main__':\n","    # Bind to localhost on port 5000\n","    app.run(host='127.0.0.1', port=5000)"],"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Writing app.py\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"0ea2f513","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1794479073950,"user_tz":-60,"elapsed":3148,"user":{"displayName":"THERESA TERFA","userId":"01331411577232298609"}},"outputId":"ff60b3ef-cbf3-4214-d827-21503f5b8f7b"},"source":["import subprocess\n","import time\n","import requests\n","\n","# Start the Flask app in the background\n","print(\"Launching Flask server in the background...\")\n","server_process = subprocess.Popen(['python', 'app.py'])\n","time.sleep(3) # Allow the API server a few seconds to fully boot up\n","\n","# Define API endpoint url\n","url = 'http://127.0.0.1:5000/predict'\n","\n","# Setup test cases representing two distinct profiles\n","sample_payload = [\n","    {\n","        \"gender\": \"F\",\n","        \"state\": \"FLORIDA\",\n","        \"membership_tier\": \"VIP\",\n","        \"preferred_channel\": \"Online\",\n","        \"referral_source\": \"Email Campaign\",\n","        \"frequency\": 25,\n","        \"total_spend\": 12500.50,\n","        \"avg_spend_per_order\": 500.02,\n","        \"ticket_count\": 0,\n","        \"avg_satisfaction\": 5.0,\n","        \"avg_resolution_time\": 0.0\n","    },\n","    {\n","        \"gender\": \"M\",\n","        \"state\": \"TEXAS\",\n","        \"membership_tier\": \"Standard\",\n","        \"preferred_channel\": \"In-Store\",\n","        \"referral_source\": \"Social Media\",\n","        \"frequency\": 1,\n","        \"total_spend\": 45.00,\n","        \"avg_spend_per_order\": 45.00,\n","        \"ticket_count\": 5,\n","        \"avg_satisfaction\": 1.5,\n","        \"avg_resolution_time\": 48.2\n","    }\n","]\n","\n","try:\n","    # Send POST request\n","    print(\"Sending test payload to Flask /predict...\")\n","    response = requests.post(url, json=sample_payload)\n","\n","    print(f\"\\nHTTP Status Code: {response.status_code}\")\n","    import json\n","    print(json.dumps(response.json(), indent=2))\n","\n","finally:\n","    # Clean up and terminate Flask server background process safely\n","    print(\"\\nShutting down backend Flask server...\")\n","    server_process.terminate()"],"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Launching Flask server in the background...\n","Sending test payload to Flask /predict...\n","\n","HTTP Status Code: 200\n","{\n","  \"results\": [\n","    {\n","      \"churn_probability\": 0.5087,\n","      \"is_churned_prediction\": 1,\n","      \"status\": \"Churn\"\n","    },\n","    {\n","      \"churn_probability\": 0.8557,\n","      \"is_churned_prediction\": 1,\n","      \"status\": \"Churn\"\n","    }\n","  ]\n","}\n","\n","Shutting down backend Flask server...\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"T3iPOjijSDW4"},"source":["### **1. Plain-Language Explanation of Predictors**\n","* **Loyalty & Tenure Dynamics**: The most prominent factors driving churn scores are the frequency and consistency of customer transactions. Customers who make regular, repeat purchases are highly unlikely to churn, indicating strong habitual engagement.\n","* **Monetary Value Concentration**: Customers who have accumulated high lifetime spend or have high average order values are significantly less likely to leave. Conversely, low-ticket, single-purchase customers pose the highest flight risk.\n","* **Support Friction**: Customer support activity plays a large role. A cluster of multiple support tickets combined with a dropping satisfaction score (below 4.0 out of 5.0) strongly flags a customer who has reached their breaking point and is highly susceptible to leaving for a competitor.\n","\n","---\n","\n","### **2. Real-World Consequences of Wrong Predictions**\n","* **Consequence of a False Negative (Type II Error)**: The model predicts a customer will **stay**, but they actually **churn**.\n","  * *Operational Impact*: We take no protective action. The customer leaves quietly, and we lose their ongoing revenue streams. Re-acquiring them will require expensive targeted remarketing campaigns that might not succeed.\n","* **Consequence of a False Positive (Type I Error)**: The model predicts a customer will **churn**, but they actually intended to **stay**.\n","  * *Operational Impact*: We proactively send them a high-value retention offer (e.g., standard discount, VIP service credit). While this preserves customer sentiment, it acts as a \"margin drain\" because we needlessly gave up revenue on a transaction that would have occurred organically.\n","\n","---\n","\n","### **3. Model Limitations & Sources of Bias**\n","* **The 180-day Inactivity Definition Bias**: Classifying any customer who has not ordered within 180 days as \"churned\" introduces a distinct systemic bias. This definition severely disadvantages **highly seasonal buyers** (e.g., those who buy exclusively during holiday periods once a year) or shoppers of highly durable goods. The model will flag these users as churned, potentially flooding them with irrelevant weekly discounts, which may irritate them and harm the brand.\n","* **Ticket Volume Representation Gap**: Support tickets only capture customers who take the time to complain. A major limitation is \"silent churners\"—disgruntled or disengaged customers who leave without ever opening a support ticket. Because the model relies heavily on ticket volume to gauge friction, it may fail to identify silent attrition."]},{"cell_type":"code","source":[],"metadata":{"id":"U9NtOoDbSDW5"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"7c89737c"},"source":["### **1. Plain-Language Explanation of Predictors**\n","* **Loyalty & Tenure Dynamics**: The most prominent factors driving churn scores are the frequency and consistency of customer transactions. Customers who make regular, repeat purchases are highly unlikely to churn, indicating strong habitual engagement.\n","* **Monetary Value Concentration**: Customers who have accumulated high lifetime spend or have high average order values are significantly less likely to leave. Conversely, low-ticket, single-purchase customers pose the highest flight risk.\n","* **Support Friction**: Customer support activity plays a large role. A cluster of multiple support tickets combined with a dropping satisfaction score (below 4.0 out of 5.0) strongly flags a customer who has reached their breaking point and is highly susceptible to leaving for a competitor.\n","\n","---\n","\n","### **2. Real-World Consequences of Wrong Predictions**\n","* **Consequence of a False Negative (Type II Error)**: The model predicts a customer will **stay**, but they actually **churn**.\n","  * *Operational Impact*: We take no protective action. The customer leaves quietly, and we lose their ongoing revenue streams. Re-acquiring them will require expensive targeted remarketing campaigns that might not succeed.\n","* **Consequence of a False Positive (Type I Error)**: The model predicts a customer will **churn**, but they actually intended to **stay**.\n","  * *Operational Impact*: We proactively send them a high-value retention offer (e.g., standard discount, VIP service credit). While this preserves customer sentiment, it acts as a \"margin drain\" because we needlessly gave up revenue on a transaction that would have occurred organically.\n","\n","---\n","\n","### **3. Model Limitations & Sources of Bias**\n","* **The 180-day Inactivity Definition Bias**: Classifying any customer who has not ordered within 180 days as \"churned\" introduces a distinct systemic bias. This definition severely disadvantages **highly seasonal buyers** (e.g., those who buy exclusively during holiday periods once a year) or shoppers of highly durable goods. The model will flag these users as churned, potentially flooding them with irrelevant weekly discounts, which may irritate them and harm the brand.\n","* **Ticket Volume Representation Gap**: Support tickets only capture customers who take the time to complain. A major limitation is \"silent churners\"—disgruntled or disengaged customers who leave without ever opening a support ticket. Because the model relies heavily on ticket volume to gauge friction, it may fail to identify silent attrition."]},{"cell_type":"code","source":[],"metadata":{"id":"12xhrsxHl4ns"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]}]}